Imagine three **(or three thousand)** coding agents working on the same repository.
Each has a branch,
and each branch can run its checks without waiting for the others. The work is
parallel until somebody tries to update `main`.

`main` is shared. **Sometimes even sacred.** It can accept one new commit at a
time. That is where a merge queue helps, and where a traditional queue can
become the slowest part of the system.

## The problem a queue solves

A pull request usually tests the latest commit on its own branch. Call that the
PR's **head**. The commit that lands is different: it combines that head with
the current tip of `main`.

While the PR was being reviewed, another change may have landed. The two
changes can merge cleanly as text and still fail together because they rely on
different types, APIs, or configuration. A green PR is evidence about one
snapshot of the code. It is not automatically evidence about the snapshot that
will land.

One study of agent-authored pull requests found that 79.4% overlapped in time
with another pull request from an agent on the same repository, and that
different-agent pairs had textual conflicts 41.7% of the time. [The study](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.04697v2)
is a useful picture of why this matters: agents create more simultaneous work
**(often targeting the same code paths)**, so more branches arrive at the same
integration point.

That is the problem merge queues address. A queue builds a temporary candidate
from the current base and a pull request, runs the required checks on that
combination, and merges it only when those checks pass. It repeats the process
one pull request at a time as the base branch advances.

{% mermaid label="A pull request changes from a tested branch into an integrated candidate" %}
```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A[PR head<br/>checks pass] --> C[Current main<br/>may have moved]
  C --> D[Integrated candidate<br/>main + PR]
  D --> E{Candidate passes?}
  E -- yes --> F[Merge]
  E -- no --> G[Fix or wait]
```
{% /mermaid %}

## Why the usual queue is expensive

The ordinary implementation repeats the same sequence for every pull request:

1. Build the merge candidate.
2. Run CI on it in the cloud.
3. Merge it.
4. Start again against the new base.

Those steps are serial. If six pull requests are ready, the sixth waits for
five integrated CI runs before its own run starts. A slow or failing run holds
the line, and everything behind it may need to be rebuilt.

That cost is easy to feel in a repository with agents. The agents save time by
working at the same time, then the queue asks the team to pay for the same
integration setup over and over. A study of 1.3 million GitHub Actions runs
measured mean job time at 6.3 minutes. [Its results](https://software-lab.org/publications/icse2024_workflows.pdf)
also put average annual GitHub Actions spend at $504 for paid-tier repositories.

## How thwip moves a change through the lane

We created thwip to help us deal with this problem: keep the useful safety
properties of a merge queue while reducing the amount of serial cloud work. The
basic shape is familiar. Like other merge queues, thwip has one lane for each
protected branch, chooses one eligible pull request at a time, checks the
combination against the current base, and merges only after its policy allows
it.

The difference comes after the candidate is built. A conventional queue usually
answers the question with a fresh CI run in the cloud for every candidate.
Thwip treats that as one option. It supports different merge-queue policies
based on what a team is optimizing for, and those policies can change over time.
The result is still a clean, generally fast merge queue; the table below shows
the choices.

Within those lanes, `main` and `release` stay separate. For this queue, a person
and the coding agent working for them count as one participant. The oldest
eligible pull request from each participant competes for the next turn, and the
participant served least recently gets it. One participant cannot put ten pull
requests ahead of everyone else.

The winning pull request gets a **pinned candidate**. GitHub's temporary merge
ref can change as the PR or base changes, so thwip stores the candidate commit,
its parents, and its code snapshot. The parents must still be the current base
and current PR head. If either moved, thwip discards the candidate and builds a
new one. It never runs checks for one snapshot and merges a different one.

The policy then decides where the evidence comes from:

| Profile | What happens | Trade-off |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `fast` | Reuse the signed proof already attached to the PR head. | Fastest, with no new run against the integrated candidate. |
| `fresh` | Ask the agent or agents working on the project to re-run the pinned candidate and sign a receipt. | No cloud rerun, but a project runner must be available. |
| `remote` | A pinned GitHub Actions verifier runs the candidate. | Central reference machine, with a CI run for each candidate. |
| `audited` | A project agent run is followed by a GitHub Actions rerun. | Strongest check, with both execution costs. |

The important detail is the pairing: the queue pins the candidate, and the
evidence names that candidate. In `fresh`, for example, the project agent gets
a signed assignment saying which code snapshot and suites to run. Its receipt
is accepted only if it describes that same snapshot. The queue can then accept
a run it did not perform itself.

Signed CI receipts deserve their own follow-up post. Here, their role is simple:
they let the queue verify what ran, where it ran, and which candidate it
covered.

{% mermaid label="The queue flow from selection through an evidence-backed merge" %}
```mermaid
flowchart TD
  A[Pull request is ready] --> B[Choose one turn]
  B --> C[Pin current base + PR head]
  C --> D{Still current?}
  D -- no --> C
  D -- yes --> E{Policy}
  E -- fast --> F[Reuse signed head proof]
  E -- fresh --> G[Project agent(s)<br/>run candidate<br/>and sign receipt]
  E -- remote --> H[Pinned GitHub verifier<br/>runs candidate]
  E -- audited --> I[Project agent run<br/>then GitHub rerun]
  F --> J[Merge pinned candidate]
  G --> J
  H --> J
  I --> J
  J --> K[Write signed record<br/>and release lane]
```
{% /mermaid %}

## What the queue buys you

The queue still serializes the one operation that must be serial: updating the
protected branch. Its work around that operation is more deliberate.

- **A controlled merge point.** The queue refuses a stale candidate. Profiles
  that run the integration bind their evidence to it, while `fast` records that
  it reused proof from the PR head.
- **Less cloud work.** `fast` spends no integrated CI run. `fresh` runs the
  candidate without sending the work to GitHub Actions. `remote` and `audited`
  keep the cloud check when that reference is worth the cost.
- **A fairer line.** Turns are assigned per person, and an expired or failed
  turn is deferred without losing its place.
- **An explanation after the fact.** Each movement gets a signed record. A
  reviewer can see whether a merge reused head proof, used a device receipt,
  or passed a pinned cloud verifier.

There is a real choice here. `fast` trusts the enrolled signing key and the
proof it produced; it does not claim that the integrated candidate ran. `fresh`
adds that integrated run, but depends on a project runner being available. `remote`
keeps the familiar GitHub execution model and pays its latency. The queue makes
those choices explicit instead of hiding them inside a workflow.

The result is a merge lane that keeps the branch controlled while giving a team
more than one way to pay for confidence: reuse evidence, run locally, or run in
the cloud.

